.TH FILETEST 1
.SH NAME
filetest \- regression test for file(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B filetest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Filetest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/file.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(mimeflag,\ path) ,
and checks it against expected output for fourteen cases, each built
from a real file under
.BR /filetest ,
cleaned up whether or not it fails. Every expected result is derived
directly from the classifier tables' own definitions \(em a file
starting with the four bytes 0x89 'P' 'N' 'G'
.I is
a PNG by this program's own table, checkable by constructing exactly
those bytes, no reference binary needed \(em see
.IR file (1)
and
.B file.b 's
own header comment.
.PP
Two cases worth calling out specifically:
.TP
.B T7
Exercises the preserved
.B dict[]
binary-search bug directly (see
.IR file (1)'s
own BUGS): two structurally identical two-line
.B #include
texts, differing only in which header is named. A control case naming
.B stdio.h
is correctly recognised as
.RB ( "c program" ),
since
.B "stdio"
is found by the table's search; a second case naming
.B bio.h
is not
.RB ( "short Ascii" ),
since
.B "bio"
sits out of sorted order in the table and the search silently misses
it. Confirmed sensitive with a negative control: temporarily moving
.B "bio"
to its correct sorted position flipped the second case's result to
.B "c program"
too, exactly as expected if the fix actually mattered.
.TP
.B T8
A first draft used "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" as
its English-text sample \(em a pangram, deliberately packed with every
letter including the rare ones
.RB ( VJKQXZ ),
which fails
.IR isenglish ()'s
own threshold (common letters must outnumber rare ones tenfold).
Replaced with ordinary prose after simulating the exact heuristic
against candidate text, not by guessing a fix.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/filetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR file (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I file
in-process via its
.B run
seam, which takes an already-resolved real file path directly \(em so
it does exercise real classification and file I/O, but not
.IR file 's
own
.BR init ,
its standard-input defaulting, its multi-argument column alignment, or
its window-system
.B Context
handling, none of which
.B run
goes through.
.PP
Does not exercise every classifier
.I file
has \(em the per-format sniffers most likely to need real sample files
from an external source
.RI ( isp9font ()'s
sibling-file probing,
.IR iself ()'s
architecture table,
.IR isface ()'s
ASCII-hex face-image format) are covered lightly or not at all here.
Each is still translated in
.BR file.b ,
just not independently verified to the same depth as the cases above.
.PP
.B T6
builds only one of the three tar checksum-field layouts
.IR istar ()
accepts (6 octal digits, NUL, space); see
.IR file (1)'s
own BUGS for the other two, neither exercised here.
.PP
Does not test
.IR file 's
own CLI argument parsing (that lives in
.BR init ,
which
.B run
bypasses entirely) \(em including the
.B -m
flag's own parsing, which was fixed during this same translation to
require an exact
.B "\-m"
argument rather than accepting any
.BR -m -prefixed
word
.RB ( "\-mjunk" )
as the flag and silently swallowing the rest, and to leave a bare
.B "\-"
alone as a filename rather than erroring on it, matching the
original's ARGBEGIN convention. Fixed by inspection and reasoning
about the parse, not exercised by a test here since
.B run
never goes through
.BR init .
